Lookups
Reference data and customer history.

GET
/police_stations
Every thana we deliver to, with its district.
Changes rarely. Fetch it occasionally and keep it, rather than on every order.
GET
/fraud_check/score/{phone}
A customer's delivery record: how much of it arrived, how much came back, and what other merchants have reported.
Response
{
    "status": 200,
    "phone": "01712345678",
    "delivery_ratio": 92,
    "cancellation_ratio": 7,
    "volume_band": "high",
    "volume_range": "50+",
    "delivered_count": 58,
    "cancelled_count": 5,
    "total_reports": 0,
    "fraud_categories": [],
    "score": null,
    "level": null,
    "reasons": [],
    "scoring_disabled": true,
    "doubtful_reports": false
}
The same figures the customer check on your dashboard shows. The two cannot disagree — they read the same stored record.
`delivery_ratio` and `cancellation_ratio` are whole percents of this customer's FINISHED parcels: delivered (partly delivered counts as delivered) against cancelled. Parcels still in flight are in neither.
Both are null — not 0 — for a number nothing has finished for. Null means "we do not know", and a customer nobody has delivered to yet is not the same as one who takes everything. Do not render null as a clean record.
They are each rounded down on their own, so they can add up to 99 rather than 100. That is deliberate: taking one as 100 minus the other rounds the second up, and neither a delivery record nor a cancellation record should be overstated by arithmetic.
`volume_band` is how many parcels this number has received, of those that finished: none, low (1–5), medium (6–20), high (21–200) or very_high (200+).
`volume_range` is the same count as merchants are shown it: exact below ten ("7"), then the step reached — "10+", "25+", "50+" or "100+". Null for a number nothing has finished for. Past ten only the step is given, because the count is every merchant's history with that customer and it is not one merchant's to read.
`delivered_count` and `cancelled_count` are the exact counts behind the ratios, as your dashboard's check shows them. They are filled in only while you are booking regularly — parcels on at least 3 of the last 7 days, or more than 3 approved in the last 24 hours — and are null otherwise. Null is "not shown", not 0.
`fraud_categories` is what the reports against the number were about, as code => times reported, worst first — for example {"no_response": 3, "refused": 1}. Reports filed before categories existed carry none, so `total_reports` can be higher than these add up to.
SCORING IS DISABLED. `score` and `level` now always answer null, `reasons` is always empty, and `scoring_disabled` is true. The keys are kept so that an integration reading them keeps parsing rather than breaking — null was always a documented answer here — but they will not carry a number again. Read the ratios and the band instead.
Why it went: one figure out of 100 made the decision and gave you nothing to check it against. A merchant refusing an order wants to know what is wrong — the split, the reports, what those reports said — and none of that survives being compressed into a single number.
Built from every merchant's history with that number, not only your own orders.
Rate limited per merchant against your own parcel volume: roughly four checks for every parcel you booked on your busiest day in the last week, plus ten. Over it, you get 429. The limit is shared with the older counts endpoint.
A signal, not a verdict. A cancelled parcel is not proof of anything, and none of this is built to say otherwise.
Already calling GET /fraud_check/{phone} for parcel counts? Move to this endpoint before 27 September 2026. On that date the old path stops returning counts, so an integration reading total_delivered and total_cancelled will find them gone. It sends a Deprecation and Sunset header in the meantime.
Status reference
Delivery statuses
Anything ending _approval_pending is the rider's word, not ours yet. Do not settle your books on it — wait for delivered, partial_delivered or cancelled.

pending	Booked and with us. Not yet attempted.
in_review	Just created and awaiting our approval. Every parcel starts here.
hold	Held — usually an address or a payment question. Contact support.
delivered_approval_pending	The rider marked it delivered; our accounts team has not confirmed it yet.
partial_delivered_approval_pending	The rider marked it partially delivered, awaiting confirmation.
cancelled_approval_pending	The rider marked it cancelled, awaiting confirmation.
unknown_approval_pending	Awaiting confirmation, outcome not one of the above.
delivered	Delivered and confirmed. The COD is now owed to you.
partial_delivered	Part of the order was delivered and confirmed.
cancelled	Not delivered and confirmed. The parcel comes back to you.
exceptional	Lost, damaged or otherwise off the normal path. Contact support.
unknown	Not a state this API recognises. Treat as "ask us".
Return statuses
Only from /status_with_return_status_by_cid/{id}, and only for a parcel that did not fully arrive.

partial_delivered_return_proccessing	Part delivered; the rest is being prepared for return.
partial_delivered_return_rider_assigned	Part delivered; a rider is bringing the rest back.
partial_delivered_return_received	Part delivered; you have the rest back.
cancelled_return_proccessing	Cancelled; the parcel is being prepared for return.
cancelled_return_rider_assigned	Cancelled; a rider is bringing it back.
cancelled_return_received	Cancelled; you have it back.
Keeping up without polling
Status answers are cached for 60 seconds, so asking more often than that costs you requests and tells you nothing. Give us a webhook URL instead and we will tell you when a parcel moves, when a return list is accepted and when a payout is made.

What the response codes mean
Code	What it means	What to do
200	Done, or answered.	Some endpoints report a per-item failure inside a 200 — always read the body.
400	The request was not understood.	Usually a malformed or missing `data` payload on a bulk call.
401	Not authenticated, or not yours.	Wrong or revoked credentials — or a parcel that belongs to another profile. See Authentication.
403	Authenticated, but not allowed right now.	An inactive account, or ownership (KYC) details that need resubmitting.
422	A field was rejected.	Read `errors` for the field names.
429	Too many requests, or too many failures.	Back off. Repeated bad credentials lock the client out — see Authentication.
500	Our fault.	Retry with the same invoice; duplicate invoices are refused, so a retry cannot double-book.